    "case_id": "113",
    "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man with no significant medical history presented with a 10-month history of spontaneous, progressively painful umbilical discomfort. A few days after the onset of pain, he noted a firm nodule at the umbilicus that gradually increased in size and became ulcerated and tender. On examination, he was in good general condition. The umbilical lesion measured 6 cm by 6 cm, was ulcerated over its entire surface, and bled on contact. Deep palpation of the abdomen revealed a poorly defined, firm mass approximately 8 cm in diameter in the right hypochondrium. Biopsy of the umbilical lesion demonstrated a well-differentiated, infiltrating adenocarcinoma. Contrast-enhanced abdominal CT showed homogeneously enhancing umbilical tissue growth and irregular, circumferential thickening of the right third of the transverse colon measuring approximately 8.3 cm. Total colonoscopy revealed no endoluminal lesion.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. jejunal adenocarcinoma\nB. based on CT thickening but ruled out by negative endoscopy\nC. an irregular, circumferential thickening in the right third of the transverse colon…Total colonoscopy did not reveal\nD. Primary umbilical malignancy less likely given higher prevalence of metastases\nE. Benign umbilical lesions excluded by malignant histology\nF. Colonic carcinoma initially suspected based on CT thickening but ruled out by negative endoscopy\nG. Gastric and pancreatic primaries considered because they are common sources of umbilical metastases but no imaging\nH. Small\n",
